<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
	The v2 Android plugin registration (Godot 4.2+): the engine scans the merged
	manifest for `org.godotengine.plugin.v2.<PluginName>` meta-data and
	instantiates the named class. No .gdap entry does this — the .gdap next to the
	AAR only tells Gradle where the binary is.

	The plugin declares its own permissions and receivers so that manifest merging
	keeps it self-contained (doc 13 §2.6). **That is true, including for the four
	`<uses-permission>` elements — re-measured on built binaries, Wave 14.** The
	2026-08-21 annotation that used to sit here said the opposite; it was wrong,
	and it is reversed rather than deleted because the wrong reading cost a
	session. The 2×2, `aapt2 dump permissions` on four locally built debug APKs:

	    AAR declares 4 | export_presets.cfg declares 4 | APK requests
	    ...............+..............................+.............
	         yes       |             yes              |      4   <- shipped
	         yes       |             no               |      4
	         no        |             yes              |      4
	         no        |             no               |    * 0 *

	**Either source suffices and the failing build had NEITHER.** The bottom row
	reproduces the phone reading exactly — zero requested permissions, plugin and
	both receivers merged, `componentsDeclared=6` — so the APK on the Fold was
	built against an AAR that predated this permission block. That is the SAME
	stale-AAR root cause the session before it had just diagnosed, and it is why
	the AAR is tracked now.

	`export_presets.cfg` carries the four as well (Wave 14), and the redundancy is
	the point: a stale or hand-edited AAR can no longer silently drop a permission,
	because the preset is a second, committed, diffable source that the exporter
	reads directly. `tests/test_release_plumbing.gd` asserts BOTH halves; do not
	delete either.

	These four are the WHOLE release manifest (doc 13 §2.7), and the list is
	enforced on every release build by `tools/make_release.sh`, which reads it
	back off the built AAB and APK rather than off any source file.

	  POST_NOTIFICATIONS      runtime (API 33+)  every notification
	  RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED  normal             re-arm alarms after a reboot
	  VIBRATE                 normal             the P1 channel's vibration + haptics
	  WAKE_LOCK               normal             Godot's keep-screen-on while playing

	Deliberately absent, and each absence is load-bearing: INTERNET (the reason the
	Play Data Safety form can say "collects nothing"), SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM and
	USE_EXACT_ALARM (Play restricts them to clocks and calendars — a city builder
	asking for one risks the listing, which is why every alarm here is inexact),
	FOREGROUND_SERVICE* (there is no service; the sim never runs in the background),
	and any storage or ad-id permission. Absent from BOTH sources now: no preset
	sets a flag for any of them either, and the test scans for that.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />
	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.VIBRATE" />
	<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.WAKE_LOCK" />

	<application>
		<meta-data
			android:name="org.godotengine.plugin.v2.SlacumNative"
			android:value="com.slacumcity.nativeplugin.SlacumNative" />

		<!--
			Both receivers are `exported="false"`: nothing outside this package has
			any business firing a Slacum notification, and the alarm one is reached
			through an explicit PendingIntent held by the system, which does not
			need export. `directBootAware` is false by design — the schedule file
			lives in credential-encrypted storage, so replay waits for unlock.
		-->
		<receiver
			android:name="com.slacumcity.nativeplugin.AlarmReceiver"
			android:exported="false">
			<intent-filter>
				<action android:name="com.slacumcity.nativeplugin.FIRE" />
			</intent-filter>
		</receiver>

		<receiver
			android:name="com.slacumcity.nativeplugin.BootReceiver"
			android:exported="false">
			<intent-filter>
				<action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
				<action android:name="android.intent.action.QUICKBOOT_POWERON" />
			</intent-filter>
		</receiver>
	</application>

</manifest>
